#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073417 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95e51046-fc4c-536b-91eb-82c7c298b7be" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Product as a Repeated Addition"}] :question/id #uuid "a3241956-5b5b-5760-9383-d7da13f48b16" :question/math-academy-id "e-10548" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fd479debb08b47c49552e543d2a8a53d0cdcbfb59c9a43333b7650f33bd063a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Write down this multiplication as a repeated addition of fraction models." :question/worked-solution "In the model, we have $\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{3}$ multiplied by ${\\color{blue}3}.$ This means that we need to make $\\color{blue}3$ copies of the given fraction model.\n\n![](images/38/38d0f24ab003913a30051d64dda233980173939825d9cc6f34473b1cbdcdbeed.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the missing fraction model is:\n\n![](images/ac/ac8618a503eb2cfd41a7fa8851cdb0dbb7152325100ec922b533d9da6e63b7c5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e7017e-45df-5adf-9ded-3f815b4f5673" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Unit Fraction by a Whole Number Using Repeated Addition"}] :question/id #uuid "eea25500-f78e-544f-8bf9-7eaacfac84a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10549" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5aef67e5f151465f09eba64456f4566cc984c97857d8e6203cd25d60952e183a.png)\n\n \n\nUse the model above to calculate $3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we need to multiply $\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{5}$ by $\\color{blue}3$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}3$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe41721739d82df825d2a7e28bc19590fbc784354a58aa74137aceda0e6be202.png)\n\n \n\nThere are ${\\color{blue}3} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 3$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore, \n\n$$\n3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5} = \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073419 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23f5f4f6-9d8c-5ab5-ada8-1212832de40f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Multiplication Problem Given a Model"}] :question/id #uuid "1c074473-dc1c-56c6-825b-be4b6b3dbfa2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10550" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e71724d5a2f16bd88fdae52e2717ec65176ff82bc74e92cd7e18a2a19f1b8218.png)\n\nWhat multiplication problem is represented by the model above?" :question/worked-solution "We have $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{4}{9}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/ee/eef3cec4311b03e7f4be59e367cbd3d6947afd73b16a00b487f28ae7ea2bf8b8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\times 4 = \\dfrac{4}{9}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can swap the factors on the left-hand side. So, the following would be a correct answer too:\n\n$$\n4 \\times \\dfrac{1}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073420 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bf2fa4e-c47e-5d89-a4aa-8e255050f792" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Missing Factor Given a Model"}] :question/id #uuid "3e10607a-5812-50cd-a2d5-4b7c3ebcbde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10555" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e9bc9f6b014b092a363dbac6ff64bedefe26846c34851fd5029c89af61d68d8f.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the multiplication problem above?" :question/worked-solution "We have $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/c1/c1efa667a938907fcdd5ba9d0172b37a7efb0a7b312e3025556c4444814d2ab3.png)\n\nThe shape on the left has $1$ shaded piece, and the shape on the right has $3$ shaded pieces. So the number of shaded pieces has increased by a factor of ${\\color{blue}3}.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5} \\times {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95e51046-fc4c-536b-91eb-82c7c298b7be" :kno